---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { supportEmail } from '../content/product';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout title="Privacy">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">YOUR LEARNING, YOUR DATA</p>
    <h1>Privacy in plain language.</h1>
    <h2>Without an account</h2>
    <p>
      Your course choices, theme, attempts, confusion markers and assignment
      checklists, walkthrough position and review ratings stay in your browser.
      You can export or import them in settings. Clearing browser storage
      removes local data. GitHub Pages may log visitor IP addresses for its own
      service security.
    </p>
    <h2>With an account</h2>
    <p>
      atlas stores a username, a securely hashed password, session records and
      learning events on the configured atlas server. Events synchronize your
      progress across devices, including saved assignment drafts where the
      feature supports them. This private sync data is separate from product
      analytics. Review events contain word IDs and ratings, not typed Japanese
      answers. This is independent learning evidence, not a school grade.
    </p>
    <h2>Optional aggregate analytics</h2>
    <p>
      Analytics are off by default. If you opt in, atlas records public page,
      course, material and feature IDs, review scope, release version, broad
      device class, result counts and coarse active-time buckets. It never
      collects typed answers, search terms, tutoring content, photos, error
      messages, passwords or tokens. Active time excludes hidden or unfocused
      tabs and pauses after 60 seconds without interaction.
    </p>
    <p>
      Random first-party guest analytics IDs expire after 30 days. Private
      product events and sessions are kept for at most 30 days, with expired
      records removed when new events arrive or an admin report is generated.
      Product dashboards and copyable reports contain aggregates without names,
      account IDs, emails or feedback messages. Administrators can separately
      see usernames, creation and activity dates, account roles and server sync
      totals to operate accounts. Registration does not collect an email. Opting
      out stops new analytics; existing older unidentifiable aggregate counts
      cannot be separated into individual contributions.
    </p>
    <h2>Requests</h2>
    <p>
      Help requests store the text you choose to submit, request category,
      optional course and optional reply email. The compact feedback control
      also includes the public course, unit, assignment, question, walkthrough
      step and page you were viewing; it does not capture typed answers or other
      browser activity. Unsent feedback drafts stay in this tab’s session
      storage and are cleared from the form after sending. Please do not include
      grades, private classroom data or assessed answers. Attachments are not
      accepted. Public GitHub issues are visible to others.
    </p>
    <h2>Deletion and shared devices</h2>
    <p>
      Signed-in users can delete their account using a password confirmation in
      settings. This removes server events, sessions, daily account activity and
      linked requests and product sessions, plus this browser’s account store.
      Other devices may retain local copies until you clear them. Server backups
      may retain deleted records until their retention window expires; the
      operator must apply deletion requests when restoring a backup.
    </p>
    <p>
      Guest requests without an account need a separate deletion request to{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a>. Password recovery
      is not available yet. On shared devices, sign out and clear local data as
      appropriate.
    </p>
    <h2>External resources</h2>
    <p>
      Teacher websites, calendar links and learning references have their own
      policies. No third-party video embeds or remote fonts load automatically.
      This atlas release links source material rather than mirroring copyrighted
      worksheets, transcripts or textbooks.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={`${base}/account/`}>Open your settings →</a> ·{' '}
      <a href={`${base}/help/`}>Need help with your data?</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
